printf
Usage: printf FORMAT [ARG]...

Format and print ARG(s) according to FORMAT (a-la C printf). Unlike echo,
it adds no newline of its own -- you write one where you want one -- and
its behaviour is identical in every shell, which is why it is the right
choice for anything with escapes or leading dashes.

Escapes in FORMAT:

	\n	newline
	\t	tab
	\r	carriage return
	\\	a literal backslash
	\0NNN	the byte with octal value NNN

Conversions, each consuming one ARG:

	%s	the argument as a string
	%d	as a signed decimal integer
	%x	as lowercase hex
	%c	as a single character
	%%	a literal percent sign

Width and alignment go between % and the letter: %5d right-aligns in
five columns, %-12s left-aligns in twelve.

	printf 'hello\n'
	printf '%s is %d bytes\n' "$f" "$n"
	printf '%-12s %s\n' name value
	printf 'GET / HTTP/1.0\r\n\r\n' | nc example.org 80

FORMAT is reused until the arguments run out, so `printf '%s\n' a b c`
prints three lines.

See also: echo, read
